My project

I'm building a procedural dungeon crawler that runs in a terminal using Python's standard library.

The dungeon is represented by a 2D grid:

# = wall
. = floor

The grid is initialized using a nested list comprehension, for example:

WIDTH = 30 HEIGHT = 15

WALL = "#" FLOOR = "."

grid = [[WALL for x in range(WIDTH)] for y in range(HEIGHT)]

Rooms are represented as tuples in this format:

(x, y, width, height)

Example:

ROOMS = [ (3, 2, 10, 6), (14, 3, 5, 5), ]

I have a create_room(grid, room_x, room_y, room_width, room_height) function that carves out rectangular rooms by changing grid cells to FLOOR.

I have a display(grid) function that prints the dungeon:

def display(grid): for row in grid: print("".join(row))

I have implemented create_horizontal_corridor(grid, x1, x2, y) and create_vertical_corridor(grid, y1, y2, x). Both functions handle reversed coordinates by swapping their endpoints and use range() with an inclusive endpoint (end + 1).

I have also written this function to connect two points with an L-shaped corridor:

def connectrooms(grid, pointa, pointb): x1, y1 = pointa x2, y2 = point_b

createhorizontalcorridor(grid, x1, x2, y1) createverticalcorridor(grid, y1, y2, x2)

This makes a horizontal corridor followed by a vertical corridor at x2.

Where we are right now

We have just finished learning how to carve rooms and connect two room centers using corridors.

The last thing we discussed was testing the L-shaped connection with points that have different y-coordinates, for example:

connect_rooms(grid, (8, 5), (16, 7)) display(grid)

We have not yet moved on to automatically connecting all rooms.

What I want to do next

Continue from this exact point. First, help me test and verify connect_rooms(). Then guide me through automatically connecting consecutive rooms using their centers, so I can generate a dungeon with multiple connected rooms.

After that, continue incrementally toward a complete game:

Improve procedural room generation and prevent room overlaps.
Connect all generated rooms.
Add the player character and movement.
Add collision detection with walls.
Add stairs and multiple floors.
Add enemies and turn-based combat.
Add loot, potions, health, and experience.
Improve the dungeon with features such as enemy pathfinding and fog of war.

Important: Do not implement all these features at once. Continue as a hands-on mentor, asking me to write each small piece myself and reviewing my attempts before moving on.

